WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
GOOGLE EDUCATION:
Schools are ditching Google from all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:08.280
fronts. Up until the pandemic, Google Clasroom&nbsp;
interest was increasing year after year. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:13.800
since 2020, interest in Google Classroom has&nbsp;
struggled to even reach levels of 2015. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:19.860
many schools are opting for third party solutions&nbsp;
like Schoology, Canvas, and Blackboard. It’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.660
just Google’s virtual classrooms that schools are&nbsp;
ditching either, schools are also ditching Gmail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.460
A quick Google search will bring up dozens of&nbsp;
articles from school newspapers detailing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.920
change. Here’s a few examples. If you look up&nbsp;
the opposite, however, not only do you find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:41.400
fewer results but the results you do find are&nbsp;
often from 2010. And as for the oh so famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:47.760
chromebooks. These used to be the go to device for&nbsp;
many school districts but more recently schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.400
have started to complain about them. Apparently,&nbsp;
chromebooks have very short life span making them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:59.580
a rather bad investment. This is a very bad sign&nbsp;
not just for Google’s educational business but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:05.280
their office suite as whole. Here’s the thing,&nbsp;
everyday people virtually never pay for Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.040
Drive or Google Docs or Gmail because the free&nbsp;
tier is more than enough for most people. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.300
much of them money that Google and Microsoft&nbsp;
make from such products come from corporations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:21.000
and schools. Google was never really able to break&nbsp;
into the corporate market with their office suite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.620
but it did seem like they were breaking into&nbsp;
the school market. The collaboration features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.060
and online nature of Google’s office suite made&nbsp;
it perfect for team projects and presentations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.380
Combine this with the pandemic and you would&nbsp;
think that schools would be paying Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.840
more than ever, and that was the case at the&nbsp;
beginning. When schools didn’t know where to go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.480
they turned to a familiar brand leading to&nbsp;
chromebook sales rocketing by 87% between 2019&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:50.940
and 2020. But, now that schools have had time&nbsp;
to become familiar with Google’s shortcomings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:57.720
and research other solutions, it appears that a&nbsp;
popular choice is to ditch Google and here’s why.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:03.480
MICROSOFT DOMINATES:
To understand why schools are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.280
suddenly ditching Google, we first have to take a&nbsp;
look back at why Google became so popular amongst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.580
schools in the first place as it was definitely&nbsp;
not because it was the best option. I mean there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:20.100
really no comparison between Microsoft Office and&nbsp;
G Suite. Though G Suite had a few unique features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.140
like easy collaboration and autosaving onto the&nbsp;
cloud, Microsoft Office was the clear winner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:30.780
And this is why corporations never really jumped&nbsp;
on board with G Suite. They were looking for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:36.180
best tools available. Not to mention, people were&nbsp;
already familiar with the Microsoft Office Suite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.820
so going with Microsoft was a no brainer. For&nbsp;
schools, however, the choice wasn’t as clear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:47.700
Sure, Microsoft was better but what real value&nbsp;
would children especially in elementary school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:54.360
get from Excel or Word. The reality was not much.&nbsp;
I remember, back when I was in kindergarten and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:00.540
1st grade, our class would go to the computer lab&nbsp;
just to practice typing. As we moved into 4th and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.520
5th grade, we would write and revise our essays&nbsp;
on paper. But, when we were ready for the rough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.580
draft, we would switch over to computers. But&nbsp;
that was really the extent of computer use. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:17.100
safe to say, Microsoft Office was quite overkill&nbsp;
for most school applications but it wasn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:23.160
Office that was overkill, it was really the whole&nbsp;
computer experience. Most students had no use for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:28.860
a full on Windows machine or Outlook email client.&nbsp;
In fact, this just made students harder to manage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:34.140
Instead of working on the final draft of an essay,&nbsp;
students would just jump onto Armour Games or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:39.960
Miniclip. So, giving children access to a computer&nbsp;
was often more of a distraction than a tool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.900
Not to mention, Windows and Office were quite&nbsp;
expensive. Schools often had to shell out $100+ to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:51.840
Microsoft alone with each computer. Clearly, what&nbsp;
schools needed was a cheaper watered down version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.240
of the whole computer experience. This seems like&nbsp;
the perfect opportunity for Google to step in with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:02.700
the G Suite and that’s what they tried doing in&nbsp;
2006 but it wasn’t as successful as you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.260
think. While Google’s offerings did mesh better&nbsp;
with the needs of schools, it wasn’t something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.720
that schools really paid too much attention to.&nbsp;
After all, each school only had 1 or 2 computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:19.020
labs, so paying a premium for 25 or 50 computers&nbsp;
wasn’t the biggest concern. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:24.060
schools had full control of when they would&nbsp;
incur these costs. They could just stick around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:30.420
with Windows XP and Word 2003 as long as they&nbsp;
wanted. So, in the late 2000s, schools weren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:35.520
exactly jumping up and down to switch to Google&nbsp;
but all of this would change with the 2010s.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.760
GOOGLE BREAKS IN:
Google’s popularity within the educational space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:48.060
can really be summed up by one trend: schools&nbsp;
aiming for a student to device ratio of 1:1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:54.480
Suddenly, buying Windows licenses wasn’t just a&nbsp;
matter of purchasing 25 or 50. Elementary schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.500
themselves had to buy several hundred. Middle&nbsp;
schools and high schools had to buy thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.360
and colleges had to buy tens of thousands. Some&nbsp;
richer schools continued to shell out money for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:08.880
Microsoft licenses but this didn’t last for long&nbsp;
as Microsoft would shoot themselves in the foot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:16.020
In 2010, Microsoft would introduce a subscription&nbsp;
model for Office called Office 365 and this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:21.480
eventually become the only option. Really, the&nbsp;
only reason for this move was that a subscription&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:26.460
model was more lucrative for Microsoft than a&nbsp;
licensing model. Most corporations would just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.260
pony up the cash and jump onto the subscription&nbsp;
model because for professional use, the G Suite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:36.240
wasn’t really a viable option. For schools,&nbsp;
however, Google’s offering started to seem a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.220
more attractive. Google’s offerings were not only&nbsp;
cheap starting at just $3 per student per year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:46.200
but you could even get it for free if your school&nbsp;
met certain qualifications. Around the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:51.660
Google would also come out with the Chromebook&nbsp;
which was also perfect for schools. Schools didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:56.940
have to worry about kids doing anything too crazy&nbsp;
with Chromebooks because they couldn’t do much to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.560
begin with. It turned out that Chromebooks and&nbsp;
the G Suite were the exact watered down versions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:05.880
of computers that schools were looking for.&nbsp;
And before you knew it schools were switching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:11.100
to Google left and right. This isn’t to say&nbsp;
that they ditched Microsoft completely. Schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:16.320
might’ve still had a few dozen Windows and Office&nbsp;
based computers in computer labs. But, as for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.580
hundreds and thousands of computers that were&nbsp;
being issued to students, these were of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:26.820
chromebooks. So, when Google launched Classroom&nbsp;
in 2014, it’s no wonder why it became the go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:32.220
option for most schools. But, this hayday for&nbsp;
Google education only lasted for a few years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:38.640
By the late 2010s, teachers and students started&nbsp;
noticing quite a few shortcomings. You see, Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.860
Classroom was nothing more than a UI to share and&nbsp;
manage Google Drive documents and presentations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:49.080
When a student turns in a document, literally the&nbsp;
only thing that’s happening is that the sharing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.520
permissions are being changed. The document gets&nbsp;
shared with the teacher’s drive and the student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:59.340
loses editing permissions. That’s pretty much&nbsp;
it. This in itself was a pretty crappy solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:04.140
because students could no longer edit the document&nbsp;
once it was turned in which didn’t really make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:09.480
sense in a lot of situations. Also, there were&nbsp;
no robust systems for self grading assessments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:15.420
announcements, gradebooks, online discussions,&nbsp;
or meetings just to name a few. These are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:21.120
basic features that are available in every other e&nbsp;
learning solution. But, despite the shortcomings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.800
most schools put up with Google throughout&nbsp;
the late 2010s because it was cheap and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.900
was mostly sufficient for their needs. But&nbsp;
all of this would change with the pandemic.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:34.680
GOOGLE GETS LEFT BEHIND:
When the pandemic hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:40.500
there was a massive influx of schools switching&nbsp;
to chromebooks and Google Classroom because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:46.020
that’s what was popular. But as the pandemic&nbsp;
raged on, schools simply started investing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:51.660
better solutions and the reasoning isn’t rocket&nbsp;
science. Virtual classrooms were no longer an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:56.910
extension to in person classrooms. For a period&nbsp;
of time, they were a full on replacement to in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.910 --> 00:08:02.280
person classrooms. And even with the shift back&nbsp;
to in person classes, the utilization of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:07.080
e learning platforms is larger than ever. In&nbsp;
fact, it seems that the in many schools, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:12.840
in person aspect is now the extension. Nowadays,&nbsp;
every homework, every quiz, every test, and every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:18.300
project is completely online. The instructions are&nbsp;
posted online, the students work together online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:23.940
and the project is turned in online. Basically&nbsp;
everything other than the teacher’s lecture itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:28.800
can be found on Canvas or Schoology or whatever&nbsp;
platform the school is using. At universities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:33.900
even the lectures are automatically recorded and&nbsp;
uploaded onto these platforms. In terms of cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:40.200
Canvas does have a free tier, but that’s mostly&nbsp;
for small scale implementations. For example, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:45.380
you were running a yoga class or a math tutoring&nbsp;
service, you might use the free tier. For district&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:51.480
wide and university wide implementations, Canvas&nbsp;
is not free, it’s actually quite expensive. Canvas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:56.520
doesn’t have any sort of set pricing and each&nbsp;
school is handled on a case by case basis. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:01.380
here are some of the numbers that are floating&nbsp;
around online. It seems that on average, schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.340
have to pay an upfront fee of several thousand&nbsp;
dollars to setup the initial system plus about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.340 --> 00:09:10.980
$25 per user per month. This could be a higher or&nbsp;
lower depending on what level of integration and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:16.380
branding a given school is looking for but that's&nbsp;
a ball park number. As you can see, that’s several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:21.660
times more than Google’s $3/month and free tiers&nbsp;
but systems like Canvas are also several times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:26.220
more effective than Google’s offerings. And given&nbsp;
how heavily these systems are now utilized at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:31.080
schools, it simply doesn’t make sense to go with&nbsp;
a watered down versions from Google. The same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:36.060
logic applies to chromebooks as well. Students&nbsp;
no longer go to the computer lab once a week to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:41.760
practice typing. They use computers the entire&nbsp;
day at school and after school as well. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.260
they’re doing increasingly complicated tasks&nbsp;
as well. Nowadays, students are 3d modeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:50.460
in engineering classes and coding algorithms in&nbsp;
computer science classes even in middle school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:56.820
Also, every job in the world uses windows or mac,&nbsp;
so it really doesn’t make sense to train students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:01.860
to use chromebooks. Some districts are even&nbsp;
going as far as purchasing Macbooks and iPads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:07.080
for students. But even those that aren’t going&nbsp;
that far are very much seeing a resurgence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:11.940
Windows and Office and more professional tools&nbsp;
in general even though they’re more expensive.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.600
THE STATE OF GOOGLE:
In the end, it seems that Google’s greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:22.440
advantage within the educational space has&nbsp;
also become their greatest weakness. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:26.640
tech adoption was still relatively early stage&nbsp;
within schools, Google was able to make a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:30.780
splash by offering tech for extremely cheap&nbsp;
whether that be chromebooks, the G suite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:35.640
or Google Classroom. While these options had&nbsp;
their shortcomings, when the virtual classroom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:41.040
was simply an addition to in person learning,&nbsp;
they held up alright. But virtual learning is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:46.260
front and center, the whole Google ecosystem&nbsp;
starts to fall apart. We should also mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:50.460
that school districts are now being run by&nbsp;
a newer generation that better understands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:54.840
tech. They realize that investing in good tech&nbsp;
for students is way more valuable than buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.840 --> 00:11:00.180
pencils and papers and desks or even building&nbsp;
new buildings. The ROI is simply incomparable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:04.920
And that’s why schools are giving up on&nbsp;
Google. They’re simply leaning towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.780
more professional solutions that make more sense&nbsp;
in our increasingly tech driven world. Education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:14.580
may not be the only realm in which Google is&nbsp;
losing ground. They may also be losing ground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:19.020
with YouTube with their new adblock policies.&nbsp;
If you wanna know why, check out this video.

